\details{
The comparison is deliberately two-sided. \strong{Agreement} is the correlation
between the two surfaces and the ratio of their totals -- if those are tight
the choice of family does not matter for the product, and a tie can be left
as a tie. \strong{Spikiness} is the rest of it, and is the half worth reading
first: two surfaces can correlate at 0.99 and still put wildly different
amounts of the total into a handful of cells, because that correlation is
carried by the tens of thousands of cells where both models predict almost
nothing.

The spike measures are scale-free on purpose (\code{max_over_med}, \code{n_spikes},
\code{gini}), so a family predicting twice the abundance is not thereby recorded
as twice as spiky. Two cautions come with them. The ones divided by
\code{median_pos} go meaningless on a near-empty surface, where that denominator
goes denormal -- \code{NL_EXPL_DRL_RA} RAZO Fall has a median positive cell of
2.0e-13 and so a \code{max_over_med} of 1.5e12 off a maximum of 0.31 birds/km^2 --
which is why \code{median_pos} is returned beside them and should be read with
them. And none of them catches a surface that has blown up \emph{everywhere}:
that is what \code{map.limit} is for.
}
\section{What this found on NL_EXPL_DRL_RA}{

Petrels Winter, fitted with \code{tw()} and the model \code{final.dsm.models} names, so
the surface \code{03.70} ships: \strong{2,267 cells above \code{MAX_DENS_VALUE}}, together
holding 99.9997\% of the seasonal total, peaking at 1.7e10 birds/km^2 for a
seasonal total of 3.95e12 birds. The \code{nb()} finalist on the same segments and
the same grid gives 6.3e6. The two surfaces correlate at 0.037.

The existing concentration check does not see it. \code{pct_top_n} for that
surface is 14.9\%, far below the level \code{03.70} warns on, because the blow-up
is spread over thousands of cells instead of piled into one -- and
\code{MAX_DENS_VALUE} is applied only when drawing maps, so every one of those
cells is absent from the picture and present in the file. A concentration
measure asks whether one cell dominates; \code{n_over_limit} asks whether many
cells are impossible, and only the second question had a useful answer here.

Concentration is where the response family stops being a detail, which is why
this exists: where one observation dominates, Tweedie and negative binomial
disagree about how likely a huge count is, and the totals diverge
accordingly. See \code{\link[=summarise.prediction.concentration]{summarise.prediction.concentration()}} for the \verb{Atl IMRP}
Herring Gull case that established the pattern.
